  never have to use the system they they  come up with a design then they throw it  over the fence and  somebody else builds it and they don't  have to use it they throw it over the  fence and then the end user takes over  and so it's it's really critical so the  cdio  it's a way of approaching engineering  particularly complex systems that from  the very beginning  you take into account all of those  phases of  an engineering system i mean it's not  not just  engineering systems when you're you  build a house  i mean you know you better figure out  what it's going to be  to live in this house before it gets  built otherwise you're going to have a  lot of  you know bills with a contractor to make  changes at the last minute and that's  one of the other things you learn  in one of the basic principles of  systems engineering  and particularly in the space business  is that the further you get into  the implementation of the system the  more expensive it it  is to make any changes i feel like  that's one of the beauties of this  course  you might be working in a completely  different discipline but you can find  something useful  that is kind of the subtext of the  course is  it's not just about the space shuttle  it's learning  how a very very complex system  was initially conceived designed  implemented and operated and that's why  after the central part of the course  where we talk about the design and the  building of all these complex subsystems  then we brought in people who actually  operated the shuttle the  the launch director the flight director  the payload operator  an astronaut pilot who flew the shuttle  and and so on because  that's the final phase of the whole cdio  is from as i say from soup to nuts this  is what the shuttle was all about  to really understand the development of  the space program here in the us  it helps to look at the interconnected  series of systems that built it  and they go well beyond engineering and  mathematics  for example p